  "metadata": {
    "title": "A negative-curvature normal barrier on an algebraic cone that is not a hyperbolicity cone",
    "upload_type": "publication",
    "publication_type": "preprint",
    "description": "<p>An explicit affirmative answer to Tun\u00e7el's 2017 existential problem: the regular algebraic convex cone K={x,y,z\u22650:xy\u00b2\u2265z\u00b3} has the 100-normal barrier F=-25 log(z(xy\u00b2-z\u00b3)) with D\u00b3F(a)[h,u,u]\u22640 for every interior base point a, every cone direction h and every real direction u, but K is not the hyperbolicity cone of any homogeneous polynomial in the original three-dimensional space. The proof supplies exact polynomial and positive-semidefinite certificates for all directions, including degeneracies, and an arbitrary-degree boundary divisibility and nonreal-root obstruction.</p><p>Concise research note with TeX source, two independently authored standard-library exact verification programs, mathematical audits and a bounded primary-source priority audit. The underlying cone and known self-concordant power-cone barriers are credited to Hildebrand and Roy\u2013Xiao; the barrier parameter is deliberately nonoptimal. The broader every-regular-cone barrier question is outside the result. AI tools were used extensively in development, verification and writing. Unrefereed; no external human peer review or formal proof-assistant certification is claimed. The priority screen does not certify exhaustive literature coverage or first-ever priority.</p>",
